%[text] # MathWorks Automotive Conference and Model Referencing
%[text] [![](text:image:6447)](https://matlab.mathworks.com/open/github/v1?repo=simulink/blog&project=2026_22_06_MAC_MdlRef/MathWorks_Automotive_Conference_MdlRef.prj)
%[text] I recently attended the [MathWorks Automotive Conference](https://www.mathworks.com/company/events/conferences/automotive-conference-michigan/2026.html). This was a great opportunity to connect with industry experts and see how they use MATLAB and Simulink.
%[text] During the conference, I gave a presentation titled [Building Efficient and Fast Models: Sharing User Experience](https://www.mathworks.com/videos/building-efficient-and-fast-models-sharing-user-experience-1779076022313.html). I thought some readers may find the topic useful, so I decided to summarize the main points here.
%[text] You can find the recording and slides here:
%[text] - Recording: [Building Efficient and Fast Models: Sharing User Experience](https://www.mathworks.com/videos/building-efficient-and-fast-models-sharing-user-experience-1779076022313.html)
%[text] - Slides: [Building Efficient and Fast Models: Sharing User Experience - PDF](https://www.mathworks.com/content/dam/mathworks/mathworks-dot-com/company/events/conferences/automotive-conference-michigan/2026/na-mac-2026-mathworks-building-fast-models.pdf) \
%[text] ## Key takeaways
%[text] I would summarize the key takeaways as:
%[text] - Componentization using Model Reference Accelerator mode is key to performance
%[text] - Know your workflows to determine what and when to componentize using Model Reference Accelerator mode
%[text] - Foundation for successful componentization \
%[text] ![](text:image:4677)
%[text] ## Know your workflows
%[text] I always like to refer to the workflows described in this technical article: [Improving Simulation Performance in Simulink - MATLAB & Simulink](https://www.mathworks.com/company/technical-articles/improving-simulation-performance-in-simulink.html).
%[text] ![](text:image:475c)
%[text] The most important recommendation to keep in mind based on those workflows is:
%[text] - **If a model is not structurally modified between simulations, it should not be recompiled.** \
%[text] ## Multiple workflows in one simulation
%[text] This is where Model Reference Accelerator mode comes into play. Model Reference allows a single simulation to be composed of multiple Simulink models... which means multiple workflows in one simulation: 
%[text] ![](text:image:4870)
%[text] ## Foundation for successful componentization
%[text] Once you realize how and when Model Reference Accelerator mode can benefit your workflows, it's time to dig into the specifics of what is needed to adopt it.
%[text] When creating a model that I plan to accelerate, or converting an existing subsystem to an accelerated referenced model, I usually go through a series of steps or questions.
%[text] ### 1 - Can it run as a referenced model in Accelerator mode?
%[text] Some features are not supported by Model Reference Accelerator mode.
%[text] I will not go through the full list here, but here are a few examples:
%[text] - Blocks that run MATLAB code in interpreted mode are not supported for Accelerator mode code generation
%[text] - Goto and From blocks cannot cross model boundaries. \
%[text] You can get the full list of limitations here: [Model Reference Requirements and Limitations](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/ug/model-referencing-limitations.html)
%[text] ### 2 - Can I tune parameters without rebuilding the accelerator target?
%[text] To benefit from Model Reference Accelerator mode, the parameters you care about must remain tunable without triggering a rebuild. For the majority of parameters, it just works by default. In some cases, you need to do a bit of extra work. 
%[text] Here are examples of the use cases I encounter the most frequently:
%[text] - **Variant Subsystems:** By default the [variant activation time](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/var/activate-variant-during-different-stages-of-simulation-and-code-generation-workflow.html) parameter is set to **`update diagram`**. To be tuned in accelerator mode, the variant activation time needs to be set to **`startup`**. \
%[text] ![](text:image:6b78)
%[text] - **Simscape:** Simscape parameters have a Configurability setting that needs to be set to Run-time: \
%[text] ![](text:image:5d50)
%[text] - **Mask Initialization code:** By default, Simulink does not generate accelerator code for mask initialization MATLAB code. For your mask initialization to preserve tunability, you need to follow the syntax described here: [Preserve Tunability of Parameters That Are Modified or Created in Mask Initialization](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/ug/preserve-tunability.html). \
%[text] ### 3 - Is the accelerator target initializing as fast as possible?
%[text] Once compiled and the cache is available, a referenced model in accelerator mode should start instantly, in less than one second. If you see longer initialization times, it's worth investigating. 
%[text] To better understand how cache and accelerator rebuilds work, look at this blog post: [Accelerator Cache and Rebuilds – Everything you need to know](https://blogs.mathworks.com/simulink/2023/10/18/accelerator-cache-and-rebuilds-everything-you-need-to-know/)
%[text] In MATLAB R2026a, a [detailed breakdown of the model initialization has been added to the Simulink Profiler](https://www.mathworks.com/help/simulink/release-notes.html?s_tid=CRUX_lftnav#mw_b85549ac-893e-4f76-b016-0f6e2824967b). We expect this newly added information to be very useful.
%[text] If you still have concerns, reach out to [technical support](https://www.mathworks.com/support/contact_us.html) and we can help you with that.
%[text] ## Now it's your turn
%[text] Are you already using Model Reference to speed up your simulations? 
%[text] If you have an existing model that you think could benefit from Model Reference, try getting help from an AI Agent like Claude Code or Codex. I had good success with the example in this blog post: [Refactoring a Simulink Model using the MATLAB MCP Server](https://blogs.mathworks.com/simulink/2026/03/26/refactoring-a-simulink-model-using-the-matlab-mcp-server/).

